    {
      "id": "p0217-b002",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Children in English-speaking regions are taught that \"please\" and \"thank you\" are \"magic words\" that should be used when making a request or after receiving something. The joke here is that \"please\" and \"thank you\" are out of place in this situation - they aren't actual \"magic words\" that magicians chant.",
        "raw": "Children in English-speaking regions are taught that \"please\" and \"thank\nyou\" are \"magic words\" that should be used when making a request or after\nreceiving something. The joke here is that \"please\" and \"thank you\" are out of\nplace in this situation - they aren't actual \"magic words\" that magicians chant.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.09665879,
          0.08906429,
          0.69683608,
          0.15599286
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.09427944,
          0.08835,
          0.69886972,
          0.15706429
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          2,
          3,
          4,
          5
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "英语地区的孩子从小会被教导：“please”（请）和“thank you”（谢谢）是提出请求、接受东西后该说的“魔法词”。这里的笑点在于，这两句话放在这种场合不合适——它们可不是魔法师真正念的咒语。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
      "annotations": [],
      "layout": {
        "bbox": [
          0.09648593,
          0.08763571,
          0.69683608,
          0.15956429
        ],
        "font_size": 20,
        "line_height": 1.45,
        "font_weight": 450,
        "background": "transparent",
        "text_align": "justify",
        "auto_height": true,
        "limit_bottom": 0.2671428571428571,
        "snap_to_guides": true,
        "resize_anchor": "left"
      },
      "calibration": {
        "bbox": [
          0.09648593,
          0.08763571,
          0.69683608,
          0.15956429
        ],
        "font_size": 20,
        "leading": 29.0
      },
      "review_issues": []
    },
    {
      "id": "p0217-b003",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Because \"please\" and \"thank you\" aren't traditionally called \"magic words\" in Japanese, the translator changed Madjick's \"please and thank you\" to popopopo-n. This mostly meaningless word is a reference to Aisatsu no Maha (\"Greetings Are Magic\"), a magic-themed public service announcement that constantly aired on Japanese television in 2011.",
        "raw": "Because \"please\" and \"thank you\" aren't traditionally called \"magic words\"\nin Japanese, the translator changed Madjick's \"please and thank you\" to\npopopopo-n. This mostly meaningless word is a reference to Aisatsu no Maha\n(\"Greetings Are Magic\"), a magic-themed public service announcement that\nconstantly aired on Japanese television in 2011.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.09631308,
          0.17077143,
          0.68769488,
          0.25718571
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.09427944,
          0.17005714,
          0.69886972,
          0.25825714
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          6,
          7,
          8,
          9,
          10
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "日语传统上不把“请”和“谢谢”叫作“魔法词”，因此译者把 Madjick 的“please and thank you”改成 popopopo~n。这个词本身基本没有意义，引用的是 2011 年在日本电视上反复播出的魔法主题公益广告《Aisatsu no Mahō》（问候的魔法）。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
