    {
      "id": "p0217-b006",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "One such \"filler video\" was Aisatsu no Maha (\"Greetings Are Magic\"), a 2010 children's song and television public service announcement that aired with brain-searing regularity in 2011. Aisatsu no Maha repeated on Japanese television so often that it became a widely shared cultural experience. The video grew so grating over time that it spawned hate memes, spooky urban legends, and elaborate conspiracy theories.",
        "raw": "One such \"filler video\" was Aisatsu no Maha (\"Greetings Are Magic\"), a\n2010 children's song and television public service announcement that\naired with brain-searing regularity in 2011. Aisatsu no Maha repeated\non Japanese television so often that it became a widely shared cultural\nexperience. The video grew so grating over time that it spawned hate\nmemes, spooky urban legends, and elaborate conspiracy theories.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.12331978,
          0.42646429,
          0.65829865,
          0.52096429
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.12199792,
          0.42575,
          0.65962052,
          0.52203571
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          18,
          19,
          20,
          21,
          22,
          23
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "其中一段就是《问候的魔法》：这首 2010 年制作的儿歌兼电视公益广告，在 2011 年以令人洗脑的频率播出。它反复出现，成为日本人广泛共有的文化记忆。时间久了，视频令人越来越烦，催生了恶搞、恐怖都市传说，以及复杂的阴谋论。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
