    {
      "id": "p0217-b009",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "As a song, Aisatsu no Maha was intended to teach children the importance of words like konnichi wa (\"hello\"), itadakimasu (\"I humbly accept this food\"), and arigata (\"thank you\"). In the song, these phrases are referred to as \"magic words\" that can help you make friends. The lyrics also include the occasional popopopo-n, a made-up sound effect word that was intended to represent \"the sound of lots of friends appearing\". Popopopo-n was such an unavoidable phrase that it won first place in 2011 's Japanese Internet Buzzword Awards.",
        "raw": "As a song, Aisatsu no Maha was intended to teach children the\nimportance of words like konnichi wa (\"hello\"), itadakimasu (\"I humbly\naccept this food\"), and arigata (\"thank you\"). In the song, these phrases\nare referred to as \"magic words\" that can help you make friends. The\nlyrics also include the occasional popopopo-n, a made-up sound effect\nword that was intended to represent \"the sound of lots of friends\nappearing\". Popopopo-n was such an unavoidable phrase that it won first\nplace in 2011 's Japanese Internet Buzzword Awards.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.12492636,
          0.74920714,
          0.67993656,
          0.87800714
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.12360449,
          0.74849286,
          0.68125842,
          0.87907857
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          30,
          31,
          32,
          33,
          34,
          35,
          36,
          37
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "这首歌原本想让孩子明白 konnichi wa（你好）、itadakimasu（我怀着感激接受这顿饭）和 arigatō（谢谢）等用语的重要性。歌词称它们为能帮你交朋友的“魔法词”，还不时穿插 popopopo~n——一个虚构的音效词，表示“许多朋友出现的声音”。它变得无处不在，甚至获得了 2011 年日本网络流行语大奖第一名。",
        "status": "draft",
